  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 8
      - mark 1 B 13
      - mark 1 C 21
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Handling One-Shot Interrupts}

      With basic {[cu]Gpio} and {[cu]ConsoleUart} services in place, we'll pivot to handling HW interrupts inside a {[cu]OneShot} driver.

      <div class="em-info">

      Managing interrupts indeed raises the bar for **EMS** dis&shy;tros in general &ndash; especially when supporting new MCUs.

      </div>

      To simplify matters, we'll examine a {[cu]OneShot} driver {[bm,A]} used throughout **Basic Examples**&thinsp; {[tr,101/04/01]} and&thinsp; {[tr,101/05/01]}.

      Note that the design of {[cu]OneShot1P} directly uses (distro-supplied) {[cn]GlobalInterrupts}{[bm,B]} and {[cn]Idle}{[bm,C]} modules.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - fold 2 3 7
      - fold 2 8 12
      - fold 2 13 14
      - fold 2 15 17
      - fold 2 19 21
      - fold 2 25 52
      - fold 2 53 69
      - fold 2 71 73
      - fold 2 74 83
      - fold 2 84 88
      - mark 2 A 8
      - mark 2 B 13
      - mark 2 C 15
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% Additions}

      Starting with a baseline {[cu]BoardC} viewed in the last tour {[tr,103/05/02]}, supporting {[cu]OneShot} requires minimal additions.

      Notice how the modules imported by {[cu]BoardC} now fall into _three_ categories, based on their bucket of origin:

      {[BM,A]} portable &ndash; {[cu]Common}, {[cu]Poller}<br>
      {[BM,B]} MCU-specific &ndash; {[cu]Idle}, {[cu]OneShot}<br>
      {[BM,C]} ARM-generic &ndash; {[cu]GlobalInterrupts}, {[cu]UsCounter}

      With multiple **EMS** distros supporting a range of **ARM** processors, {[cs]'@em.arch.arm'} has earned its keep.

      <div class="em-info">

      In the limit, we want to _minimize_ each distro's unique contributions and _maximize_ re-use of generic elements.

      </div>
      

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - fold 4 18 100
      - mark 4 A 4
      - mark 4 B 14
    focus: [4, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot% Driver &ndash; <code><b>em$meta</b></code>}

      This implementation of the {[cu]OneShotI} in&shy;terface imports the {[cs]'@rpi.distro.2040'} {[cu]REGS} facade as {[cs]$R}{[bm,A]}.

      Targeting the {[cn]TIMER}.{[cn]ALARM0} peripheral, {[cu]OneShotAlarm0} must manage interrupts sourced by the underlying MCU.

      <div class="em-info">

      This implementation therefore imports {[cu]IntrVec} &ndash; first seen in the context of {[cu]BuildC} {[tr,103/03/04]}.

      </div>

      Invoked at build-time&thinsp;{[bm,B]}, the {[cf]useIntr} meta-function tells {[cu]IntrVec} that we'll handle {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'} interrupts.

      A name unique to this MCU, let's return to {[cu]BuildC} where we'll find the original declaration of {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'}&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Interrupt Declarations}

